

1. MicroMutations — small parameter shifts

These are tiny, continuous changes to scalar values.They keep universes stable while exploring nearby law‑space.

def micro_mutation(genome, rate=0.1, scale=0.05):
    for key, value in genome.parameters.items():
        if random.random() < rate:
            delta = random.uniform(-scale, scale)
            genome.parameters[key] = value + delta

What they affect:

warp_A, warp_B, warp_C

chaos_strength

nonlocal_scale

curvature_damping

mixer_temperature

These mutations are safe and frequent.

2. MacroMutations — discrete jumps in behavior

These change the shape of the physics, not just the values.

def macro_mutation(genome, rate=0.05):
    # Exponent jumps
    if random.random() < rate:
        genome.parameters["warp_p"] += random.choice([-0.2, 0.2])

    if random.random() < rate:
        genome.parameters["warp_q"] += random.choice([-0.2, 0.2])

    # Operator weight shifts
    for op, w in genome.operators["operator_weights"].items():
        if random.random() < rate:
            genome.operators["operator_weights"][op] = w * random.uniform(0.8, 1.2)

What they affect:

warp exponents

nonlocal decay exponents

operator weight ratios

mixer bias exponents

These mutations create new physics behaviors.

3. StructuralMutations — topology of the law system

These are the rare, dramatic changes.They alter the structure of the universe’s laws.

def structural_mutation(genome, rate=0.01):
    # Toggle operators on/off
    for op in ["enable_warp", "enable_nonlocal", "enable_chaotic"]:
        if random.random() < rate:
            genome.operators[op] = not genome.operators[op]

    # Add/remove mixer biases
    if random.random() < rate:
        genome.mixer_bias["warp_bias"] *= random.uniform(0.5, 1.5)

    # Regime threshold jumps
    if random.random() < rate:
        genome.regime_rules["warp_activation_threshold"] *= random.uniform(0.7, 1.3)

What they affect:

whether warp physics exists

whether chaos is allowed

whether nonlocality is active

regime switching thresholds

mixer bias topology

These mutations create entirely new universes.

4. Combined Mutation Operator

This is the mutation engine your Evolution Layer will call each generation.

def mutate_genome(genome):
    # Copy genome to avoid destructive mutation
    new_genome = genome.copy()

    micro_mutation(new_genome)
    macro_mutation(new_genome)
    structural_mutation(new_genome)

    new_genome.metadata["generation"] += 1
    return new_genome

This gives you:

small exploration

medium leaps

rare structural rewrites

Exactly what a law‑evolving system needs.

5. Mutation Rate Control (Adaptive)

You can evolve the mutation rate itself.

def adapt_mutation_rate(genome, fitness):
    if fitness < 0.3:
        genome.metadata["mutation_rate"] *= 1.1
    else:
        genome.metadata["mutation_rate"] *= 0.95

Low‑fitness universes mutate more.High‑fitness universes stabilize.

This is self‑tuning evolution.







